  "enhanced_information": "To reduce the risk of transfusion-related cytomegalovirus (CMV) infection, leukoreduction is the most effective method. CMV is primarily transmitted through blood products, particularly via leukocytes, which can harbor the virus. Leukoreduction effectively removes these white blood cells, significantly decreasing the risk of CMV transmission. While irradiation inactivates lymphocytes and can also reduce CMV risk, it is less effective than leukoreduction. Washing blood products does not effectively remove all leukocytes, and centrifugation does not significantly impact CMV risk. Warming blood products is unrelated to leukocyte content and does not reduce CMV risk. Therefore, the best option to minimize the risk of CMV infection in this scenario is leukoreduction.",
  "analysis": "The patient presents with symptoms and laboratory findings consistent with thrombocytopenia and anemia, likely due to her recent chloramphenicol treatment. In the context of blood transfusion, the risk of transfusion-related cytomegalovirus (CMV) infection is a significant concern, especially in immunocompromised patients or those with low leukocyte counts. The most effective method to reduce this risk is through leukoreduction, which removes white blood cells from the blood product, thereby decreasing the likelihood of CMV transmission. While irradiation can also help reduce the risk by inactivating lymphocytes, it is not as effective as leukoreduction in preventing CMV transmission. Other options like washing, warming, and centrifugation do not significantly impact the risk of CMV infection. Therefore, the best-supported option to minimize the risk of CMV infection in this scenario is leukoreduction.",
